<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="android:editTextBackground">@drawable/rn_edit_text_material</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      What MainActivity launches with, and what it stops using in onCreate.

      The theme on the <activity> decides what the window looks like before any
      of this app's code runs, which on a cold start is the second or two the
      bundle takes to load. It was AppTheme, whose background is AppCompat's
      default — white under a light system theme — so the wallet opened with a
      white flash and then cut to its own dark splash.

      THE SWAP BACK IS NOT OPTIONAL. MainActivity calls setTheme(AppTheme)
      before super.onCreate, and without it this drawable stays as the window
      background for the life of the process: it is what shows through
      anywhere the app does not paint, which is most visible behind a keyboard
      and during activity transitions. It would look like a rendering bug and
      read like one too.
    -->
    <style name="SplashTheme" parent="AppTheme">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/splash_screen</item>
    </style>

</resources>
